From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kristof Imeri <kristof@lanth.me>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 04:53:53 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 38/55] wayland: send Chromium builds touchpad deltas in their
 own units

Chromium multiplies every finger and continuous scroll delta it gets on
Wayland by 12, treating 10 pixels as one 120-unit wheel notch, so pages
and Electron apps scroll about twelve times as far as the fingers move.
Chromium 154 added the WaylandUnscaledTouchpadScrolling feature to stop
doing that, but it is off unless something turns it on, and Electron
apps have no way for people to turn it on.

When a client's executable sits beside chrome_100_percent.pak, read the
Chromium release from the V8 version in its snapshot and, for releases
that scale, divide its finger and continuous deltas by 12. Releases
before 154 always scale, except 143, which shipped without the scaling.
From 154 on, browsers keep their deltas, since their flags files enable
the feature, while Electron apps, recognised by the app in resources/
beside the executable, keep them only when the last --enable-features
on their command line names the feature. Each client is checked once,
when it first asks for a pointer, and kinetic scrolling follows because
Chromium derives it from the same deltas.
---
